H. After the referee
   the cell problem pooled four ways (weighted by the scatter at each height):
      slope over 0.2 to 1 of the height, heights 500 and up (26 windows; what I first quoted): 1.32591 (0.00004)
      slope over 0.2 to 1 of the height, all 34 windows: 1.32588 (0.00004)
      slope over 0.5 to 1 of the height, heights 500 and up: 1.32583 (0.00006)
      slope over 0.5 to 1 of the height, all 34 windows: 1.32582 (0.00006)
      the windows of height 200 alone: 1.32565 (0.00012); every pooled figure lies between 1.3258 and 1.3259
   do the sizes from a million up agree with each other? chi-square on 4 degrees of freedom: a_tot 10.7, a_prof 2.9, a_size 4.3
   a_size is tied to a_tot: the sizes of the layers add up to n, so if they follow c sqrt(1 - k/N) then c = 3n/(2N) and the two estimates are one. At 10^8: a_tot 1.32592, a_size 1.32592
   the spread of the count, fitted with each size weighted by its number of clouds: exponent 0.174 (0.005), chi-square 3.8 on 7
      with the exponent fixed: 1/6 gives chi-square 5.6 on 8; 1/5 gives 27.7; 2/9 gives 85.3
      from a million points up only: exponent 0.19 (0.02)
      variance of the count over n^(1/3): 0.081, 0.080, 0.079, 0.081, 0.082, 0.103, 0.095, 0.103, 0.081   (10^4 to 10^8)
      variance of the count over the square root of the count: 0.118, 0.118, 0.116, 0.119, 0.120, 0.151, 0.139, 0.151, 0.119
   the cell problem, all five window heights together: variance of depth at a height as a power of the height: exponent 0.52 over heights 20 to 4000, 0.59 over 1000 to 4000, 0.52 over 20 to 1000
      variance of depth over the square root of the depth: 0.61 at depth near 100, 0.63 near 1000, 0.69 near 4000
   Calder and Smart, Theorem 2.12, bound the fluctuation of the depth at height r in the cell problem by (log r)^3 r^(1/2); what I see, about r^(0.26), is inside that
